{
  "case_id": "2020-19-357",
  "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Craig Goldblatt",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDBLATT: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nThis case presents the question whether the Bankruptcy Code's automatic stay requires a creditor in lawful possession of estate property when a bankruptcy petition is filed to return that property to the debtor immediately or else pay damages.\nIt does not. The automatic stay freezes the state of affairs as of the bankruptcy filing by enjoining creditors from taking post-petition acts to improve their position vis-a-vis the debtor. As this Court has repeatedly observed, the automatic stay's purpose is to preserve the status quo. It does not require creditors to turn over property lawfully in their possession."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Just -- just in ordinary language, though, if you're holding the property, you're acting to exercise control. Just ordinary language."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, a --a potential difference that might matter between the two provisions is that 362 is automatic, and it's disputed whether 552 -- 542 is automatic."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SINZDAK",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0001-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SINZDAK: I -- I think so in -- insofar as you can say that the automatic sanctions provision of 362 does not attach to 542. I think Respondents seem to believe that 362 is the only way to get automatic sanctions, and that, presumably, is why they're advancing this case. But -- but that's not a reason to sort of start distorting Section 362 to read it in different ways."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents (Eugene R. Wedoff)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "These cases present a single question: May a creditor stop a Chapter 13 debtor from recovering property that the creditor seized before the bankruptcy filing if the debtor does not have a court order directing the return? For three reasons, reflected in the Court's questions today, the creditor may not prevent recovery of the property."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, if --if the two provisions work together, wouldn't you expect that -- that there'd be a cross-reference?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, to follow up on Justice Breyer's question, if you are correct that 542 is self-executing, then I don't really understand your answers to his questions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, where in 362(a)(3) do you see a requirement that the debtor has to turn it over only upon request?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What would be your then next step in Congress or wherever is appropriate and what kind of legislation to ensure the purpose I spoke about?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So I just thought I'd ask, is there -- is there any other circumstance in which the automatic stay provision compels an action that alters the status quo, or is -- is -- is -- is this one the only one in your view?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "City of Chicago",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the City of Chicago here and the SG, too, argues that the only thing that the automatic stay provision is about is maintaining the status quo."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In '84, they added the \"or exercise control\" provision. And isn't there some linguistic gap in there? For better or worse, Congress never talked about just retaining possession, it didn't speak about keeping possession, it didn't speak about anything else about possession."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel, I'm sorry, this is Justice Sotomayor. I don't understand that last point. If a turnover provision under 542 is -- would result in the debtor coming in and saying, I need the property to earn a living, under 542, you can come in and say: I need security or raise any other sorts of defenses that would delay the proceedings."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-19-357",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3149228e4fe1974762f8e719a51eea777063f77260152ac0b6a7d07f75de7819",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3149228e4fe1-0003-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 80,
        "start": 80
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it's important to bear in mind, as this Court's opinion in Whiting Pools explains, that the Bankruptcy Code strikes a careful balance between the rights of secured creditors on the one hand and the rights of debtors to marshal the assets of the estate on the other and provides specific rights and protections to secured creditors."
    },
    {
